    <h1>Grímnismál Stanza 20</h1>
    <p class="lore-subtitle">The Only Verse That Names Huginn and Muninn</p>
  </header>

  <section class="lore-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><em>Grímnismál</em> stanza 20 is the sole surviving Old Norse verse to name both of Odin's ravens together. It survives in the <em>Codex Regius</em> (c. 1270 CE) and reads: "Huginn and Muninn fly every day over the vast earth; I fear for Huginn that he may not return, yet I fear more for Muninn."</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>The Manuscript and Poem</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> <em>Grímnismál</em> ("The Sayings of Grímnir") is an Eddic poem preserved in the <em>Codex Regius</em> (GkS 2365 4°), an Icelandic manuscript dated to around 1270 CE, now held at the Árni Magnússon Institute in Reykjavík.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup> The poem is universally considered older than the manuscript; most scholars date its composition to before 1000 CE, though the exact date is unknown.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p>In the poem, Odin is captured and tortured by King Geirröðr, who does not recognise him. Disguised as "Grímnir" (the Masked One), Odin reveals cosmological secrets to the king's young son Agnarr — the stanzas that form the bulk of the poem. Stanza 20 falls in this sequence of cosmic revelation.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>The Old Norse Text</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The stanza as it appears in the <em>Codex Regius</em>:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <blockquote class="lore-quote lore-quote--old-norse">
      <p lang="non">
        Huginn ok Muninn<br/>
        fljúga hverjan dag<br/>
        Jörmungrund yfir;<br/>
        óumk ek of Hugin,<br/>
        at hann aftr né komi-t,<br/>
        þó sjámk meir of Munin.
      </p>
      <footer>— <em>Grímnismál</em>, stanza 20, <em>Codex Regius</em> GkS 2365 4°</footer>
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>Word-by-Word Gloss</h2>
    <p>Each phrase explained (Hrafn's own glossing, following standard Old Norse lexicography):<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
    <table class="lore-gloss-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Old Norse</th><th>Literal meaning</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td lang="non">Huginn ok Muninn</td><td>Huginn and Muninn</td><td>"Thought" and "Memory/Mind"</td></tr>
        <tr><td lang="non">fljúga hverjan dag</td><td>fly every day</td><td><em>fljúga</em> = to fly (infinitive after modal); <em>hverjan dag</em> = "every day" (accusative)</td></tr>
        <tr><td lang="non">Jörmungrund yfir</td><td>over the vast earth</td><td><em>Jörmun-</em> = "vast/mighty" (cf. <em>Jörmungandr</em>); <em>grund</em> = earth, ground; <em>yfir</em> = over (postposition)</td></tr>
        <tr><td lang="non">óumk ek of Hugin</td><td>I am afraid for Huginn</td><td><em>óask</em> = to fear (middle voice); <em>of</em> = concerning</td></tr>
        <tr><td lang="non">at hann aftr né komi-t</td><td>that he may not come back</td><td>subjunctive <em>komi</em> + enclitic <em>-t</em> (negation); <em>aftr</em> = back, again</td></tr>
        <tr><td lang="non">þó sjámk meir of Munin</td><td>yet I fear more for Muninn</td><td><em>þó</em> = yet/still; <em>sjámk</em> = "I see/fear" (here idiom for anxiety); <em>meir</em> = more</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section">
    <h2>Translation and Commentary</h2>
    <p>A close prose translation (Hrafn's own, following Larrington 1996 and Faulkes 1987):</p>
    <blockquote class="lore-quote">
      <p>"Huginn and Muninn fly every day over the vast earth; I fear for Huginn that he may not return, yet I fear more for Muninn."</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The stanza is metrically in <em>ljóðaháttr</em> ("song metre"), the form typically used for gnomically weighty statements in Eddic poetry. Its brevity — six lines — belies its density: the stanza establishes the ravens' daily range, their role as observers, and Odin's dependency on them, all in a few words.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p>The anxiety about Muninn is the stanza's most discussed element. Carolyne Larrington notes in her commentary (1996) that memory in Norse thought was not simply recollection but carried connotations of "desire" and "intention" — which makes Muninn's loss more catastrophic than Huginn's.<sup><a href="#src-4">4</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="lore-section lore-bridge">
    <h2>The Birds Behind the Story</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Common Ravens range widely — often many kilometres in a day — in search of food, and their intelligence, pair-bonding, and apparent curiosity about human activity would have been familiar to anyone living in Scandinavia or Iceland.<sup><a href="#src-5">5</a></sup> The poem's claim that Huginn and Muninn "fly every day over the vast earth" maps onto the raven's real behaviour: wide daily ranging across open country.</p>
    <p><a href="/species/common-raven/" class="lore-species-link">Common Raven — species profile →</a></p>
    <p><a href="/lore/norse/huginn-and-muninn/" class="lore-species-link">Huginn and Muninn — full analysis →</a></p>
  </section>

